Strategy: Construct a racemic ornithine skeleton by conjugate hydrocyanation of acrolein, capture the aldehyde as a hydantoin, and only then reduce the remaining side-chain nitrile. This ordering avoids selective partial nitrile reduction, free 4-aminobutanal, and guanidination of an unprotected alpha-amino group. Five guanyl donors are evaluated at two genuinely different masking stages: the intact hydantoin and its ring-opened N-alpha-carbamoyl derivative. These combinations give ten distinct conditional pathways, not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic syntheses; methyl/ethyl donor pairs are homologous alternatives. No supplied evidence establishes an end-to-end prebiotic arginine synthesis. Numerical conditions below are proposed experimental screening windows, not reported substrate-specific yields or proven geological operating windows. The principal unresolved requirements are selective acrolein hydrocyanation, activity of a natural nickel-bearing phase toward the protected nitrile, donor availability, guanidination selectivity, and productive nonenzymatic unmasking. Simple feedstocks are stipulated rather than assigned unsupported origins. All reactions are anoxic. Surface organics would require transport through water-filled fractures into a sulfur-poor hydrogen-rich hydrothermal reaction zone, followed by cooling and concentration before guanidination; final acidic hydrolysis requires a separate chloride-rich geothermal compartment. Such transfers are physical assumptions requiring recovery measurements, not additional synthesis reactions. Molecules are represented once in neutral constitutional forms; protonation, counterions, and acid-base neutralization are described in conditions rather than duplicated as molecule nodes. Formation-environment labels identify a principal formation setting, not an exclusive one. The network describes productive transformations, not an exhaustive inventory of competing products.
