  P1: Copper-assisted cyanosulfidic glycolaldehyde Strecker route
    Description: Formaldehyde and HCN supply glycolonitrile; Cu(I)/H2S photochemistry supplies glycolaldehyde; Strecker capture and staged hydrolysis yield racemic serine. Strong evidence supports the precursor-forming reactions, while mild-condition hydrolysis efficiency remains a bottleneck.
    Reactions: rxn_001 -> rxn_002 -> rxn_007 -> rxn_009 -> rxn_010
    Key intermediates: mol_006, mol_007, mol_014, mol_015

  P2: Copper-free cyanosulfidic glycolaldehyde Strecker route
    Description: The same carbon construction proceeds without added copper during photoreduction. This removes one catalyst-supply requirement but does not inherit the copper-assisted rate or yield; hydrolysis and photochemical over-reduction remain constraints.
    Reactions: rxn_001 -> rxn_003 -> rxn_007 -> rxn_009 -> rxn_010
    Key intermediates: mol_006, mol_007, mol_014, mol_015

  P3: Cyanosulfitic bisulfite-reservoir Strecker route
    Description: Sulfite/ferrocyanide photochemistry stores reduced carbon as the experimentally observed glycolaldehyde bisulfite adduct. Release into a less sulfite-rich stream permits Strecker capture. Useful adduct release and complete conversion to free serine have not been demonstrated as an integrated sequence.
    Reactions: rxn_001 -> rxn_004 -> rxn_005 -> rxn_007 -> rxn_009 -> rxn_010
    Key intermediates: mol_006, mol_012, mol_007, mol_014, mol_015

  P4: Cyanosulfitic cyanohydrin-reservoir Strecker route
    Description: The alternative observed carbon reservoir is glyceronitrile rather than the bisulfite adduct. Ammonia-driven equilibration and Strecker capture are proposed to convert that reservoir to serine aminonitrile. The reservoir is experimentally supported; its productive downstream conversion is a conditional extension.
    Reactions: rxn_001 -> rxn_006 -> rxn_008 -> rxn_009 -> rxn_010
    Key intermediates: mol_006, mol_013, mol_014, mol_015

  P5: Strecker glycine followed by copper-promoted hydroxymethylation
    Description: Formaldehyde Strecker chemistry and hydrolysis first produce glycine. A separate alkaline, sulfide-poor Cu(II)-containing stage adds another formaldehyde carbon to produce serine. Residual cyanide must not be assumed compatible with the copper stage.
    Reactions: rxn_011 -> rxn_012 -> rxn_013 -> rxn_016
    Key intermediates: mol_017, mol_018, mol_019

  P6: Strecker glycine followed by pyridoxal-aluminium hydroxymethylation
    Description: Strecker-derived glycine enters an experimentally supported pyridoxal-Al(III) serine-forming reaction. This is a chemically supported comparator pathway, not a geochemically closed route, because pyridoxal supply and soluble aluminium complexation remain unresolved.
    Reactions: rxn_011 -> rxn_012 -> rxn_013 -> rxn_017
    Key intermediates: mol_017, mol_018, mol_019

  P7: Nickel-hydrogen glycine supply followed by copper hydroxymethylation
    Description: Supported Ni/H2 reductive amination converts supplied glyoxylate to glycine. Transfer away from nickel and hydrogen precedes Cu(II)-promoted addition of formaldehyde. This combines demonstrated reaction types without asserting that nickel directly produces serine from hydroxypyruvate.
